                        "sense_id": "c0c7b81a-810c-3b37-93b8-1d00b156f4cd",
                        "entry_id": "0a90c22d-027a-3935-b1c0-b27112d5053c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of or pertaining to the literary style and characters of plays by William Rowley (ca1585-1626), English playright."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "75e62cd5-61e2-4845-8ffd-6f1ac1832440",
                                "sense_id": "c0c7b81a-810c-3b37-93b8-1d00b156f4cd",
                                "text": "The style of which is neither Rowleian nor Shakespearean but a particular blending of both."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ebca5f1b-613f-46ec-b296-c04ecc183bd4",
                                "sense_id": "c0c7b81a-810c-3b37-93b8-1d00b156f4cd",
                                "text": "Tim [Bloodhound] shows many signs of being a typical Rowleian clown; he is a fat clown, judging from his joke about breaking his girdle."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9b05f148-08b4-380c-8411-b5ac2668e6a8",
                        "entry_id": "0a90c22d-027a-3935-b1c0-b27112d5053c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of or pertaining to the work of (fictional) poet Thomas Rowley, a pseudonym of Thomas Chatterton (1752-1770), English poet."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c0fd177e-0101-4695-a36e-923d024e8f56",
                                "sense_id": "9b05f148-08b4-380c-8411-b5ac2668e6a8",
                                "text": "Tyrwhitt pointed out that the Rowleian dialect was not English of the fifteenth century, nor of any century, but a grotesque jumble of archaic words of very different periods and dialects."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "295fb7fc-ba24-470d-af19-3403111f34a1",
                                "sense_id": "9b05f148-08b4-380c-8411-b5ac2668e6a8",
                                "text": "Mr. Tyrwhitt's book on the Rowleian controversy, which is reckoned completely victorious, are all the novelties I have seen since I left town."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9780614d-acc7-4a2b-a2cd-3f6c5bcbc27b",
                                "sense_id": "9b05f148-08b4-380c-8411-b5ac2668e6a8",
                                "text": "Chatterton... also imparted to Barrett two Rowleian poems, \"The Parliament of Sprites,\" and \"The Battle of Hastings\" (in two quite different versions)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
